  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Photo Exhibition: 30th Anniversary of IOM Switzerland</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2024, IOM Switzerland will celebrate 30 years of existence and fruitful collaboration with partners in Switzerland. The center piece of this anniversary year will be an photoexhibition portraying the main historical migration and human displacement events that occurred between 1994 and 2024 in Europe and which have affected Switzerland. The exhibition will coverof photographs of key events by the Reuters Photographer Darrin Zammit, and will be complemented by explanations and insights into the work of IOM both in Switzerland and in the world, which evolved alongside these events. It will also serve as a starting point to further explore migration issues beyond the depicted migration events to cover the whole complexity of the topic. This will be done visually, but also using QR Codes and IOM interactive communication materials and toolkits, as well as through the organization of thematic panel discussions, complementary to the exhibition. The aim is to raise awareness of the significance of the depicted events and IOM&#x2019;s mandate to ensure that migration is safe, orderly, and humane, to promote international cooperation on migration governance issues, to assist in the search for practical solutions to migration problems, and to provide humanitarian assistance to migrants in need, including refugees and internally displaced people.IOM Switzerland will curate this exhibition. This includes selecting the photos and creating the narrative around them, to give an overview of developments linked to migration and to IOM&apos;s work from a historical and geo-political point of view, on national, regional and global level. To this end, IOM Switzerland will create webpages accessible through a QR Code and will produce information material. IOM Switzerland will also collaborate with different divisions within IOM to make use of existing tools, visuals and interactive material.     The exhibition will be shown at the &apos;Kornhausforum&apos; in Bern for one month, before it is moved to the &apos;Piazza&apos; of the Swiss State Secretariat for Migration (SEM), where it will stay for two months.  After that, it may be moved to further cities within Switzerland.</narrative>
  </description>
